`#ifdef MACRO` checks if `MACRO` has been defined (e.g., by `#define` or a compiler flag). If `MACRO` is defined, the code block between `#ifdef` and the corresponding `#endif` is included in the compilation. Otherwise, it is completely ignored by the compiler.
```c
#define DEBUG_MESSAGES

void log_message(const char* msg) {
    #ifdef DEBUG_MESSAGES
        printf("DEBUG: %s\n", msg);
    #endif
}
```

Include guards prevent the same header file from being included multiple times within a single compilation unit. Multiple inclusions can lead to redefinition errors for types, functions, or variables declared in the header. They are implemented using `#ifndef`, `#define`, and `#endif`.
```c
// my_header.h
#ifndef MY_HEADER_H
#define MY_HEADER_H

// All declarations (structs, functions, etc.) go here
struct MyData { int value; };
void my_function();

#endif // MY_HEADER_H
```
